course: dictionary
lesson: 13239
title: Genuine: real and true
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Genuine» means real. Something genuine is what it seems to be, not a copy or a fake. «The shop sold genuine leather boots, not plastic copies.» The boots are real leather, so genuine must mean real.
explain: The word can also describe a person or a feeling. It means honest and truly meant. «Her smile was genuine, and it reached her eyes.» A genuine smile shows real happiness, not a pretend one.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. Genuine often sits near words like real, true or honest. Swap in the word real: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
fact: Genuine means real, or truly what it claims to be, not fake.
fact: Genuine can also mean honest and sincere, truly meant.
fact: «Genuine» is an adjective. The adverb is genuinely and the noun is genuineness.
fact: «A genuine email from the bank» is a real message; a scam pretends to be from the bank.
fact: The phrase «the genuine article» means the real thing, not a copy.
exercise: «The jeweller showed me a genuine diamond, not glass.» What does genuine mean here? | *Real, not fake | Old and dusty | Made of glass
exercise: «He gave a genuine apology, and he really meant it.» What does genuine suggest? | *Honest and truly meant | Loud and angry | Rushed and careless
exercise: Mia gets an email that says it is from her bank. Her dad says to check it is genuine. What does he mean? | Check it is written in capital letters | *Check it is real and not a scam | Check it is in another language | Check it is short
test: «The museum said the painting was a genuine work by the artist, not a copy.» What does genuine mean? | Very large | Painted in bright colours | *Real and not a copy | Sold quickly
test: Which phrase means the real thing, not a copy? | a careless mistake | *the genuine article | a fake copy | the made-up story
exam: Which sentence uses genuine to mean honest and sincere? | «The bag is genuine leather, not plastic.» | «The genuine email came from my school.» | *«Her thanks were genuine; she truly meant every word.» | «The coin was genuine silver, not tin.»
